\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (training details in Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others due to earlier convergence; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models ($\varepsilon$-free; $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies to selection only, stable across $[0, 0.2]$, Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, macro F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).
This collapse illustrates a regime where model capability is insufficient to produce meaningful inter-sample variation: 51\% of instances yield zero F1 across all samples, and consistency signals merely detect this floor rather than discriminating quality among non-trivial outputs.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

Four of five signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) assign identical scores to all candidates from the same input by construction (\S\ref{sec:formulation}); they can estimate quality across instances but cannot rank candidates within an instance.
The empirical question is whether LP, the sole per-sample signal, can exploit its candidate-discriminative nature for selection.
LP selection effectiveness is primarily determined by LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy level (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Non-tied subset in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}. $^\dagger$Instance-level signals assign one score per input; per-sample ranking $p$-values are undefined.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: quality estimation $\rho$ (seed 42); line with error bars: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean, seeds 42/123/456). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal significantly outperforms greedy (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}). Dashed green: oracle upper bound ($+13.1$~pp); dotted gray: random baseline.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Instance-level signals assign identical QE scores to all candidates; MBR conversion does not improve over greedy on the primary test bed (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best.

\paragraph{LP: the sole per-sample signal also fails.}
On SciERC ($n{=}529$, 2-seed sign tests\footnote{MDE${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power ($n{=}529$); Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$; MDE${\approx}$0.22~pp) provides the definitive test.}), the oracle (0.775) reveals 13.0~pp of headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$~pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5~pp); selection effects below this threshold cannot be reliably detected.
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); under stringent TOST anchors, some configurations show significant \textit{degradation} (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}), while structural signals still degrade selection.

\paragraph{Qualitative factors governing selection effectiveness.}
Three factors qualitatively shape LP selection gains: (1)~\textit{instance degeneracy} compresses the candidate space, leaving fewer instances where any signal can improve over greedy (SciERC 17.8\%, CoNLL 61.7\%; Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}); (2)~\textit{oracle headroom} bounds the maximum achievable gain (CoNLL ${\approx}2$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 13.1\,pp); (3)~\textit{LP-quality alignment}, measured as within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$, determines whether LP can discriminate candidates in the remaining non-degenerate instances (Few-NERD $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
These factors are consistent with the observed pattern (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}): Few-NERD (low degeneracy, strong alignment) shows the largest LP gain ($+1.39$\,pp), SciERC (weak alignment) shows unreliable gains ($+0.04{\pm}0.48$\,pp, 3-seed; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}), CoNLL (high degeneracy, minimal headroom) shows no gain, and WNUT-17 represents the signal-collapse boundary: LP $\rho{=}{-}0.446$ (the model assigns higher log-probabilities to confidently wrong outputs), within-instance alignment absent ($\rho{=}0.043$, n.s.), and construction ineffective ($p{>}0.05$, mixed sign) because 51\% of instances yield F1${=}0$.
Yet QE remains effective on WNUT-17 (VC $\rho{=}0.724$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}), confirming QE-selection decoupling at this boundary.
This characterization is qualitative: the three factors do not combine into a predictive model ($R^2{<}0$ across 23 configurations), but each factor's role is individually supported by controlled ablations (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Few-NERD/CoNLL: 3-seed means; SciERC: single-seed (3-seed in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy: $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$), while structural signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp). LP is 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp) and generalizes to LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Training convergence modulates degeneracy: 5-epoch Few-NERD raises degeneracy to 31\% and halves LP gains (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP selection direction.}
Across 23 configurations, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though within fixed settings it causally modulates LP ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
Cross-dataset prediction requires LP signal quality: Few-NERD ($\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$) shows consistent gains, SciERC ($\rho{\approx}0.26$) does not (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type analysis ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; $N$-scaling analyses are in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); signal rankings are stable across seeds ($W{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}). NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: 8B vs.\ 4B QE $\rho$ ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed 42). RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP remains below greedy ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$); temperature manipulation ($T{=}1.2$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) confirms that low degeneracy and adequate alignment are independently necessary.

\paragraph{Supervised signal combination and hidden probes.}
Supervised classifiers (LR, MLP, RF; 5-fold CV, $N{=}16$) and a DeBERTa-v3-base verifier all fail to close the selection gap (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).
Hidden state probes improve correlation ($+37$\% on SciERC) but not selection (gap closure $-5.1$\%; Appendix~\ref{app:hidden_probe}); the bottleneck extends to model-internal representations.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Scaling Behavior}
\label{sec:scaling}

We evaluate Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct in a zero-shot setting to test whether the gap persists at larger scale without fine-tuning.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Fine-tuned 8B vs.\ zero-shot 72B ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Hdr.\ = oracle $-$ greedy (pp). Degen.: instance degeneracy (\%).}
\label{tab:scale_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Regime & Greedy & Hdr. & Degen. & VC & SJ & FK & LP \\
\midrule
SciERC & 8B FT  & .644 & 13.0 & 17.8 & .379 & .360 & .267 & .205 \\
       & 72B ZS & .399 & 15.0 & 21.0 & .277 & .217 & .227 & .081 \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 8B FT & .748 & 12.4 & 13.0 & --- & --- & --- & .220 \\
         & 72B ZS & .628 & --- & 46.2 & .416 & .345 & --- & .119 \\
\midrule
CoNLL  & 8B FT  & .908 & ${\approx}$2 & 61.7 & .424 & .436 & .400 & .302 \\
       & 72B ZS & .712 & 5.3 & 67.7 & .297 & .304 & .302 & $-$.002 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP $\rho$ drops sharply without fine-tuning across all three datasets (Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}), and LP selection fails on all. Structural signals generalize to zero-shot (Few-NERD VC $\rho{=}0.416$, SJ $0.345$). Degeneracy increases (Few-NERD: $46.2$\% vs.\ $13.0$\%; CoNLL: $67.7$\% vs.\ $61.7$\%).
This comparison simultaneously varies model family (Qwen3$\to$Qwen2.5), training regime (fine-tuned$\to$zero-shot), and quantization (FP16$\to$AWQ 4-bit); while the consistency of structural signal rankings across these changes suggests the gap is robust, we cannot fully disentangle these factors.
LP-weighted construction yields $+1.53$\,pp on SciERC 72B ZS ($p{=}0.008$, CI [$+0.25$, $+2.82$]; $d{=}0.10$), recovering $10.2$\% of headroom (FT: $7.8$\% SciERC, $11.3$\% Few-NERD). Majority vote also succeeds ($+1.39$\,pp); LP-weighting adds $+0.14$\,pp. Construction fails on Few-NERD 72B ZS ($-0.20$\,pp) and CoNLL ($-0.37$\,pp), where degeneracy exceeds $46$\%---consistent with the degeneracy-gating pattern in the fine-tuned regime ($n{=}3$ datasets).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}
\label{sec:prescriptive}

The preceding sections establish that instance-level selection fails for structured IE. We now present two approaches that extract value from multi-sample generation without relying on sample ranking.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
Rather than selecting one of $N$ complete outputs, we aggregate entities across all samples, retaining those exceeding a confirmation threshold $\theta{=}2/N$ (${\geq}2$ of $N$ samples), with LP-weighted or uniform frequency scoring. Under fine-tuning, this default achieves the best or near-best F1 across all datasets ($\theta$ stable within $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In the zero-shot regime, uniform voting degrades (SciERC $-0.52$\,pp, CoNLL $-0.92$\,pp) because greater output diversity admits noise entities; LP-weighting is necessary for construction to succeed without fine-tuning (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity construction vs.\ greedy ($\Delta$ macro F1; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B, multi-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). $\theta{=}2/N$. $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. Best in \textbf{bold}. $^\ddagger$LP-weighted not computed; uniform only.}
\label{tab:entity_construction}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Wt. & Greedy & Constr. & $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
SciERC & LP & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $\mathbf{.656{\pm}.003}$ & $+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp & .004 \\
       & Unif. & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $.653{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.71$\,pp & .023 \\
\addlinespace
Few-NERD & LP & $.748$ & $\mathbf{.762{\pm}.005}$ & $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
         & Unif. & $.748$ & $.756{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.58$\,pp & .003 \\
\addlinespace
CoNLL$^\ddagger$ & Unif. & .908 & .904 & $-0.39$\,pp & n.s. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP-weighted construction improves over greedy on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$) and SciERC ($+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp, $p{=}0.004$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}), positive across all 7 seed-dataset combinations, recovering ${\approx}$8\% (SciERC) and 11\% (Few-NERD) of oracle headroom. CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with ceiling-regime degeneracy. The mechanism is recall-driven: aggregating low-frequency correct entities recovers mentions that no single sample captures ($N$-scaling in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

\paragraph{LP-alignment gating (preliminary diagnostic).}
Construction and LP selection are complementary: construction dominates on SciERC ($+1.02$\,pp vs.\ LP $+0.04{\pm}0.48$\,pp, n.s.; $\rho{\approx}0.2$), while on Few-NERD both yield comparable gains ($+1.40$ vs.\ $+1.39$\,pp; $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$).
We estimate within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$ on 50 held-out instances with bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples). Degeneracy-adjusted $\rho_{\text{adj}}$: Few-NERD $0.516$ [0.459, 0.759], SciERC $0.167$ [0.146, 0.281], CoNLL $0.065$ [$-$0.061, 0.620], WNUT-17 ${\approx}0$ (n.s.).
The protocol performs three-way routing: (1)~$\rho_{\text{adj}} \geq \tau{=}0.3$: apply LP selection; (2)~$\rho_{\text{adj}} < \tau$ and construction shows positive dev-set gain: apply construction; (3)~otherwise: retain greedy decoding.
This routes Few-NERD to LP selection ($+1.39$\,pp); SciERC, CoNLL, and WNUT-17 fall below $\tau$ and route to greedy. The conservative protocol trades SciERC's missed construction gain ($+1.02$\,pp) for protection against negative outcomes (CoNLL $-0.39$\,pp, WNUT-17 n.s.).
Routing is stable across $\tau{\in}[0.2, 0.4]$ and matches test-set direction for all four datasets; narrower inter-dataset margins remain untested.

\paragraph{Selective prediction.}
Orthogonal to construction, QE signals support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering low-confidence instances yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

